  "faq": [
    {
      "question": "What are pierogi?",
      "answer": "Polish dumplings — thin dough pockets filled with savory or sweet ingredients, boiled or pan-fried. Most popular: ruskie (potato & cheese), meat, mushroom & cabbage. A staple since the Middle Ages."
    },
    {
      "question": "Best fillings to try?",
      "answer": "Start with ruskie (potato & cheese). Then mushroom & sauerkraut, meat pierogi, or creative options like game meat with lemon at CiociaGotuje."
    },
    {
      "question": "Boiled or fried?",
      "answer": "Both great. Boiled is traditional and softer. Fried (smażone) adds crispy golden exterior. Most spots offer both — try a mix."
    },
    {
      "question": "How much do pierogi cost?",
      "answer": "15-35 PLN ($4-9) at pierogarnie. Upscale: 40-80 PLN. A filling lunch with drink: 25-40 PLN ($6-10)."
    },
    {
      "question": "Late-night pierogi?",
      "answer": "Przystanek Pierogarnia: open until 3:30 AM weekdays, 5:30 AM weekends. Zapiecek: until 11 PM (midnight weekends)."
    },
    {
      "question": "Vegetarian/vegan options?",
      "answer": "Ruskie are vegetarian, mushroom-cabbage often vegan. Prażka Bistro and CiociaGotuje are especially veg-friendly."
    },
    {
      "question": "Is Zapiecek worth it?",
      "answer": "Fine for first-timers with English menus. But locals rate GOŚCINIEC, U Kresowiaka, and KOMPOT higher. Safe default, not the best."
    }
  ],
